# Copy this file to .env.local (gitignored via the repo's existing
# .env/.env.* rule in the root .gitignore) and fill in a real value.
# NEVER commit .env.local — it is not tracked by git, and this .example
# file must never contain a real key either.
#
# Read only by apps/extension/build.mjs, at `pnpm build:extension` time —
# not by Vite, not by any other app in this monorepo. See build.mjs's own
# header comment for exactly how/where the value ends up (baked into
# background.bundle.js as a literal string, replacing the
# __FLUXIO_HELIUS_API_KEY__ identifier — never a live process.env lookup
# inside the built bundle).
#
# Optional. Leave this file absent (or this line empty) to build with the
# existing public/free Solana RPC endpoints only, exactly as before this
# key existed — the build never fails for a missing key.
#
# Get a free key at https://www.helius.dev (Dashboard -> API Keys ->
# Create New API Key). Fixes the documented prompt-0116 known issue: free
# public Solana mainnet RPC endpoints block getTokenAccountsByOwner
# outright, which breaks SPL token balances ("Tokens failed to load") in
# the extension popup on mainnet specifically (devnet/local are unaffected
# and already work without this).
#
# Security note (prompt 0130): this extension is entirely client-side —
# ANY key baked into the bundle is visible to anyone who unpacks the
# installed .crx, the same way it would be for any browser extension.
# Helius's dashboard currently only supports IP-based key restriction, not
# a domain/origin restriction that could lock a key to this extension's
# own chrome-extension:// id (confirmed against Helius's own docs as of
# this prompt) — and IP restriction wouldn't help here anyway, since a
# shared bundled key is used from every installer's own, different, often-
# dynamic IP address, not one fixed server IP. The only thing this file
# actually prevents is the key reaching the public GitHub repo; treat a
# Helius free-tier key as a low-stakes, rate-limited credential you're
# comfortable rotating if it ever needs to be, not a real secret.
HELIUS_API_KEY=

# Prompt 0170 — the Fluxio backend's base URL, used only by the
# notifications inbox (background.js polls GET /api/announcements on it,
# via chrome.alarms — see that file's own header comment). Same
# build-time-only mechanism as HELIUS_API_KEY above, for the same reason:
# a chrome.storage/env value at RUNTIME would need to reach the bundled
# background.bundle.js somehow anyway, and build-time substitution is
# already the established pattern here, not a new one invented for this
# feature. NOT a secret — this is a plain, publicly-known API origin, not
# a credential.
#
# Updated by prompt 0182 (the production domain is real now —
# https://api.fluxiowallet.com went live in prompt 0178's deploy):
# - Leave this empty (or this file absent). Dev builds (`pnpm
#   build:extension`) default to http://localhost:4000 — a real local
#   `pnpm dev:backend` — which is this feature's own safe no-op: an
#   unreachable localhost backend just means every poll fails silently
#   and the inbox stays on its last cached state (see
#   fetchAndMergeNotifications()'s own comment). Release builds (`pnpm
#   build:extension:release` / `pnpm package:extension`) default to
#   https://api.fluxiowallet.com instead.
# - An explicit value here overrides the default in BOTH modes (e.g. a
#   staging backend) — except that a release build refuses outright to
#   bake in any localhost/127.0.0.1 value (it throws; see
#   scripts/backendBaseUrl.mjs), so a dev override can never silently
#   ship in a Chrome Web Store zip.
FLUXIO_BACKEND_BASE_URL=
